description:
In repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/solicitation-outcome-scan.ts, drop solicitations whose gap is no longer open, so the outstanding-escalation count stops counting decisions that are no longer needed.

WHY. The ids this resolver scans come from stateful-ui's outstanding question panels, not from the gap store, and a panel is never retired when its gap closes. Measured 2026-09-14 against the live store: of 147 solicitations reported unanswered, 49 are MOOT — 22 name a gap whose status is closed, 5 name a rejected gap, and 5 name a gap id that no longer exists at all. The real queue awaiting a human decision is 98. That 50 percent overstatement flows straight into the operator-escalation-backlog gap this resolver's report now feeds, so the substrate tells its operator the queue is half again as long as it is, and the longest-waiting ids it lists may be for gaps that were resolved weeks ago. An escalation whose gap is closed is not awaiting a decision, so excluding it is what the report already claims to mean.

THE CHANGE. Exactly one op. Find this line, which occurs exactly once in the file:

    return { shape: "solicitationOutcomeReport", body: { authored: true, outcomes: [], answered: 0, episode_count: 0, note: "no outstanding solicitations" } };

Replace that single line with exactly these lines:

    return { shape: "solicitationOutcomeReport", body: { authored: true, outcomes: [], answered: 0, episode_count: 0, note: "no outstanding solicitations" } };
  }
  try {
    const gapsRaw = await Bun.file((process.env["WORKSPACE_ROOT"] ?? "/workspace") + "/gaps/gaps.json").text();
    const parsed = JSON.parse(gapsRaw) as unknown;
    const gapRows = Array.isArray(parsed) ? parsed as Array<{ id?: string; status?: string }> : [];
    const openIds = new Set(gapRows.filter((g) => String(g?.status ?? "open") === "open").map((g) => String(g?.id ?? "")));
    const before = ids.length;
    ids = ids.filter((qid) => openIds.has(qid.replace(/^reland-needs-human-/, "").replace(/^needs-human-/, "")));
    if (ids.length !== before) console.log("[solicitation-outcome-scan] dropped " + (before - ids.length) + " of " + before + " panels whose gap is closed, rejected or absent");
  } catch { /* gap store unreadable: fail open, scan every panel as before */ }
  if (ids.length === 0) {
    return { shape: "solicitationOutcomeReport", body: { authored: true, outcomes: [], answered: 0, episode_count: 0, note: "no outstanding solicitations with an open gap" } };

WHAT THIS CHANGES AT RUNTIME. This is not cosmetic. Today every panel stateful-ui still holds is scanned and reported, including panels for gaps that closed weeks ago, so the unanswered count and the longest-waiting list are both wrong. Afterwards a panel is scanned only when its gap id is present in the store with status open, which on today's data removes 49 of 147 entries and makes the reported queue 98. The strip of the reland-needs-human- prefix must happen before the needs-human- strip, because the longer prefix contains the shorter one. If the gap store cannot be read the filter is skipped entirely and behaviour is exactly as before, because a failed read is not evidence that a gap is closed.

SCOPE. One replacement only. Keep the original early-return line as the first line of the replacement — the block it closes is unchanged. Do not touch the stateful-ui fetch above it, readAnswersByPanel, the panel matching, or the outcome mapping below. The second early return differs from the first only in its note text, so the two cases stay distinguishable in a report.

